<li id="bib.bib24" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Williams [1992]</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
Ronald J. Williams.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">Simple statistical gradient-following algorithms for connectionist reinforcement learning.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><em id="bib.bib24.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mach. Learn.</em>, 8(3–4):229–256, May 1992.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">ISSN 0885-6125.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">doi: <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_Url ltx_ref_self">10.1007/BF00992696</span>.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">URL <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00992696" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00992696</a>.

</span></li>
      
<li id="bib.bib25" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Yue et al. [2025]</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
Yang Yue, Zhiqi Chen, Rui Lu, Andrew Zhao, Zhaokai Wang, Shiji Song, and Gao Huang.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">Does reinforcement learning really incentivize reasoning capacity in llms beyond the base model?

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><em id="bib.bib25.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">arXiv preprint arXiv:2504.13837</em>, 2025.

</span></li>
    
</ul>
</section>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Baseline Implementation Details</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide background on reinforcement learning (RL) for large language models (LLMs) and describe the objective that different baselines optimize.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>RL for LLMs</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(a\mid s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(a\mid s)</annotation></semantics></math> denote a policy parameterized by <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, which defines the probability of taking action <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> in state <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. A trajectory of length <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(s_{0},a_{0},s_{1},a_{1},\dots,s_{T},a_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(s_{0},a_{0},s_{1},a_{1},\dots,s_{T},a_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the total reward of a trajectory is <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. In the context of language models, the initial state <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the input question, actions correspond to generated tokens, and subsequent states represent the question along with the partial sequence of answer tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">RL training using Reinforce <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Williams, 1992</a>]</cite> optimizes the following objective:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\theta^{*}=\text{argmax}_{\theta}~\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}}~\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{\theta}(.\mid x)}R(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext>argmax</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\theta^{*}=\text{argmax}_{\theta}~\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}}~\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{\theta}(.\mid x)}R(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a question sampled from the distribution of questions <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is a response sampled from the LLM <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(.\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(.\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is the reward for generating response <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> to question <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. In our graph search setting, we prompt the LLM to output its final answer inside <code id="A1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">\boxed{}</code>, so the reward function simply extracts the string in <code id="A1.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">\boxed{}</code> and compares it to the true path.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Dr. GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>]</cite></h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The objective in Equation <a href="#A1.E1" title="In A.1 RL for LLMs ‣ Appendix A Baseline Implementation Details ‣ What Can You Do When You Have Zero Rewards During RL?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> typically exhibits high variance. To reduce this variance, recent methods such as GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>]</cite> Dr. GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>]</cite> subtract a baseline from the reward, which is computed by generating multiple rollouts for a given question and taking the average reward. In our setting, we perform on-policy training; thus, the importance ratio and advantage clipping terms in the objective from equation 3 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>]</cite> vanish, resulting in the following final objective:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},\{y_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{\theta}(.\mid x)}\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\sum_{t=1}^{|y_{i}|}\hat{A}_{i,t}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{i,t}\mid y_{i,&lt;t})-\beta~\mathbb{D}_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}||\pi_{\text{ref}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},\{y_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{\theta}(.\mid x)}\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\sum_{t=1}^{|y_{i}|}\hat{A}_{i,t}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{i,t}\mid y_{i,&lt;t})-\beta~\mathbb{D}_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}||\pi_{\text{ref}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x,y_{i})-\frac{1}{G}\sum_{j=1}^{j=G}R(x,y_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x,y_{i})-\frac{1}{G}\sum_{j=1}^{j=G}R(x,y_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. We set <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=5</annotation></semantics></math> for all our experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, the objective increases the likelihood of tokens that lead to positive outcomes and decreases the likelihood of tokens that do not. Note that this loss formulation weights all steps in the reasoning chain equally, i.e., <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>VinePPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kazemnejad et al., 2024</a>]</cite></h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Methods like GRPO and Dr. GRPO weigh all tokens in a reasoning chain equally. However, this may not be optimal, as not all steps in a reasoning chain contribute equally to solving a problem. For example, identifying lemmas to use in proving a theorem may be more important than generating tokens that are included merely for grammatical correctness. VinePPO addresses this problem by performing credit assignment to weigh more important steps higher and less important steps lower. It does so by computing step level advantages instead of trajectory level advantages.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a question <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\sim\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\sim\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a reasoning chain <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y\sim\pi_{\theta}(.\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\sim\pi_{\theta}(.\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>, VinePPO divides the reasoning chain <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> into steps or chunks <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{c_{1}},y_{c_{2}}\ldots y_{c_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{c_{1}},y_{c_{2}}\ldots y_{c_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and finally the gradient of the objective is computed as</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{A}^{\pi_{\theta}}_{y_{c_{i}}}\sum_{t=s_{c_{i}}}^{e_{c_{i}}}\,\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}\!\big(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t},x\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.337em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{A}^{\pi_{\theta}}_{y_{c_{i}}}\sum_{t=s_{c_{i}}}^{e_{c_{i}}}\,\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}\!\big(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t},x\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}^{\pi_{\theta}}_{y_{c_{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}^{\pi_{\theta}}_{y_{c_{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the advantage of step <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> representing the start and end indices of the <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> chunk, respectively. The advantage at each step is estimated using Monte Carlo rollouts from the state before and after that step. In other words, given a trajectory <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=y_{c_{1}}\oplus y_{c_{2}}\oplus\ldots\oplus y_{c_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=y_{c_{1}}\oplus y_{c_{2}}\oplus\ldots\oplus y_{c_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the concatenation operation <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}^{\pi_{\theta}}_{y_{c_{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}^{\pi_{\theta}}_{y_{c_{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{A}^{\pi_{\theta}}_{y_{c_{i}}}=V^{\pi_{\theta}}(\oplus_{j=1}^{j=i}y_{j})-V^{\pi_{\theta}}(\oplus_{j=1}^{j=i-1}y_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{A}^{\pi_{\theta}}_{y_{c_{i}}}=V^{\pi_{\theta}}(\oplus_{j=1}^{j=i}y_{j})-V^{\pi_{\theta}}(\oplus_{j=1}^{j=i-1}y_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi_{\theta}}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi_{\theta}}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is computed by taking the average reward recieved from <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> Monte Carlo rollouts. In our experiments we set <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=3</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that we omit the KL penalty term from Equation <a href="#A1.E3" title="In A.3 VinePPO [ , ] ‣ Appendix A Baseline Implementation Details ‣ What Can You Do When You Have Zero Rewards During RL?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for the sake of simplicity. For VinePPO, we use the code released by the authors <a href="https://github.com/McGill-NLP/nano-aha-moment/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">here</a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Progress Rewards <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Setlur et al., 2025a</a>]</cite></h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Like the VinePPO objective in Equation <a href="#A1.E3" title="In A.3 VinePPO [ , ] ‣ Appendix A Baseline Implementation Details ‣ What Can You Do When You Have Zero Rewards During RL?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, progress rewards also aim to compute step-level advantages. However, their objective differs from VinePPO in two key ways: (i) they estimate the advantages in Equation <a href="#A1.E4" title="In A.3 VinePPO [ , ] ‣ Appendix A Baseline Implementation Details ‣ What Can You Do When You Have Zero Rewards During RL?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> under a policy different from the one being optimized, which they refer to as the prover policy, and (ii) combine the step-level advantages with the trajectory-level reward to form the final objective:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}(R(y)+\alpha\cdot\hat{A}^{\mu}_{y_{c_{i}}})\sum_{t=s_{c_{i}}}^{e_{c_{i}}}\,\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}\!\big(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t},x\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.337em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}(R(y)+\alpha\cdot\hat{A}^{\mu}_{y_{c_{i}}})\sum_{t=s_{c_{i}}}^{e_{c_{i}}}\,\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}\!\big(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t},x\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{Best-of-4}(\pi_{\text{ref}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Best-of-4</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{Best-of-4}(\pi_{\text{ref}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Similar to equation <a href="#A1.E4" title="In A.3 VinePPO [ , ] ‣ Appendix A Baseline Implementation Details ‣ What Can You Do When You Have Zero Rewards During RL?" class="ltx_ref ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> they compute step advantages under the prover policy <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{A}^{\mu}_{y_{c_{i}}}=V^{\mu}(\oplus_{j=1}^{j=i}y_{j})-V^{\mu}(\oplus_{j=1}^{j=i-1}y_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{A}^{\mu}_{y_{c_{i}}}=V^{\mu}(\oplus_{j=1}^{j=i}y_{j})-V^{\mu}(\oplus_{j=1}^{j=i-1}y_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In their work, a neural network is trained to approximate <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi_{\text{ref}}}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi_{\text{ref}}}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> by collecting a dataset of reasoning trajectories sampled from <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For simplicity, we instead rely on rollouts to compute <math id="A1.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi_{\text{ref}}}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi_{\text{ref}}}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>. With this, we optimize the following objective:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}(\hat{A}(y)+\alpha\cdot\hat{A}^{\mu}({y_{c_{i}}}))\sum_{t=s_{c_{i}}}^{e_{c_{i}}}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}\big(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t},x\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>μ</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}(\hat{A}(y)+\alpha\cdot\hat{A}^{\mu}({y_{c_{i}}}))\sum_{t=s_{c_{i}}}^{e_{c_{i}}}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}\big(y_{t}\mid y_{&lt;t},x\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Notice the change from Equation <a href="#A1.E5" title="In A.4 Progress Rewards [ , ] ‣ Appendix A Baseline Implementation Details ‣ What Can You Do When You Have Zero Rewards During RL?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, where we replace <math id="A1.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(y)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>. We make this substitution because we operate in a group-style setting with access to multiple rollouts for a question. Using <math id="A1.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="A1.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(y)</annotation></semantics></math> yields a lower-variance estimate of the gradient. <math id="A1.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as <math id="A1.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R(y)-\text{average reward over $G$ rollouts})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>average reward over </mtext><mi>G</mi><mtext> rollouts</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R(y)-\text{average reward over $G$ rollouts})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Best-of-N Finetuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chow et al., 2025</a>]</cite></h3>

<div id="A1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chow et al. [2025]</a></cite> introduce inference-aware fine-tuning that explicitly optimizes for BoN performance. The hope is optimizing for BoN performance could result in the model sampling <span id="A1.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">diverse</span> responses.
A key result is Lemma 3, which provides a BoN-aware policy gradient estimator under binary rewards.
By introducing asymmetric weighting functions that depend on the probability of failure, the method upweights correct predictions on hard inputs while redistributing mass away from unreliable outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming the reward <math id="A1.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(x,y)\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x,y)\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the gradient of the BoN-aware RL objective (Equation 6) with respect to the model parameters <math id="A1.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A1.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x\sim D}\!\left[\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{\mathrm{bon},R=1}}\!\left[\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)\right]\,g^{+}_{N}\!\bigl(P_{\mathrm{fail}}(x)\bigr)-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{\mathrm{bon},R=0}}\!\left[\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)\right]\,g^{-}_{N}\!\bigl(P_{\mathrm{fail}}(x)\bigr)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>bon</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>fail</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>bon</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>fail</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x\sim D}\!\left[\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{\mathrm{bon},R=1}}\!\left[\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)\right]\,g^{+}_{N}\!\bigl(P_{\mathrm{fail}}(x)\bigr)-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{\mathrm{bon},R=0}}\!\left[\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)\right]\,g^{-}_{N}\!\bigl(P_{\mathrm{fail}}(x)\bigr)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where the sample-dependent weights are</p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{+}_{N}(p)=\frac{N\,p^{\,N-1}}{1-p^{N}},\qquad g^{-}_{N}(p)=\frac{N\,(1-p^{\,N-1})}{1-p^{N}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{+}_{N}(p)=\frac{N\,p^{\,N-1}}{1-p^{N}},\qquad g^{-}_{N}(p)=\frac{N\,(1-p^{\,N-1})}{1-p^{N}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Hyperparameters</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Appendix B Hyperparameters ‣ What Can You Do When You Have Zero Rewards During RL?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> lists the hyperparameters that are common across all methods. Since we do on-policy training parameters such as the clipping ratio (<math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>) and <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">ppo-epochs</span> are not applicable.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A2.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A2.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="A2.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameter</span></th>
<td id="A2.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="A2.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Value</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Learning Rate</th>
<td id="A2.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">Effective Batch Size</th>
<td id="A2.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r"><math id="A2.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">Number of rollouts per data point</th>
<td id="A2.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r"><math id="A2.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">Max Prompt Length</th>
<td id="A2.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r"><math id="A2.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1024</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">Max Response Length</th>
<td id="A2.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r"><math id="A2.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4096</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4096</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">KL Coefficient (<math id="A2.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<td id="A2.T1.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r"><math id="A2.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">Temperature</th>
<td id="A2.T1.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">0.6</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">Top p</th>
<td id="A2.T1.2.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">0.999</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.2.10" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.2.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">Micro Batch Size</th>
<td id="A2.T1.2.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">4</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.2.11" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.2.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">Discount Factor (<math id="A2.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>)</th>
<td id="A2.T1.2.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">1</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T1.2.12" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T1.2.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">Base Model</th>
<td id="A2.T1.2.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r">Qwen2.5/Qwen-1.5B-Instruct</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Common hyper-parameters used in our experiments.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>VinePPO</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We estimate the value of an intermediate state, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi_{\theta}}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi_{\theta}}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>, using three rollouts from the current policy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Rewarding Progress</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We estimate the value of an intermediate state, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi_{\text{ref}}}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi_{\text{ref}}}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>, using three rollouts from the reference policy. The parameter <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation <a href="#A1.E7" title="In A.4 Progress Rewards [ , ] ‣ Appendix A Baseline Implementation Details ‣ What Can You Do When You Have Zero Rewards During RL?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> is set to 5, following the recommendation in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Setlur et al. [2025a]</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Best-of-N Aware Finetuning</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> used in Best-of-N, we use <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=8</annotation></semantics></math>. We clip the sample dependent weights to <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-3,3]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-3,3]</annotation></semantics></math>. We decay the KL coefficient from 0.1 to 0.001.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Prompts</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For our experiments, we prompt the Qwen2.5/Qwen-1.5B-Instruct model in the following manner</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="136.16" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 136.16" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,136.16) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 21.07 L 0 136.16 L 528.93 136.16 C 540.57 136.16 550 126.73 550 115.09 L 550 0 L 21.07 0 C 9.43 0 0 9.43 0 21.07 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 21.07 L 1.38 113.22 L 548.62 113.22 L 548.62 1.38 L 21.07 1.38 C 10.2 1.38 1.38 10.2 1.38 21.07 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E6E6E6;" fill="#E6E6E6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 114.61 L 1.38 134.78 L 528.93 134.78 C 539.8 134.78 548.62 125.97 548.62 115.09 L 548.62 114.61 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 9.26 121.23)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.4em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="462.16"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A3.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.4em;">
<span id="A3.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Prompt for path finding problem</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 9.26 12.72)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.41em;--ltx-fo-height:6.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="96.09" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 92.63)" width="531.49"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A3.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.41em;">
<span id="A3.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Given a bi-directional graph in the form of space separated edges, output a path from source node to the destination node in the form of comma separated integers.</span></span>
<span id="A3.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For this question the graph is 81,252 97,124 285,182 97,285 97,81 124,199</span></span>
<span id="A3.p2.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The source node is 97</span></span>
<span id="A3.p2.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The destination node is 252</span></span>
<span id="A3.p2.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within <code id="A3.p2.pic1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">\boxed{}</code>.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">During training, the string inside <code id="A3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">\boxed{}</code> is extracted and compared against the ground truth path to assign the reward.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Additional Results</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Easier versions of the task are solvable: </span> To rule out any implementation issues and verify that the graph search task is not inherently unsolvable, we experiment with simpler variants of the task, such as the <span id="A4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Degree-3-Path-3</span> and <span id="A4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Degree-5-Path-5</span> graphs. As shown in Figs. <a href="#A4.F7.sf1" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Appendix D Additional Results ‣ What Can You Do When You Have Zero Rewards During RL?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a> and <a href="#A4.F7.sf2" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Appendix D Additional Results ‣ What Can You Do When You Have Zero Rewards During RL?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a>, RL training using outcome rewards is effective on these simpler variants, but remains ineffective on the harder <span id="A4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Degree-10-Path-10</span> dataset.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03971v1/rewards_Different_Graph_Datasets.svg" id="A4.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/303;" width="548" height="303"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Rewards obtained during training <span id="A4.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Dr.GRPO</span> on the graph search task with varying levels of difficulty.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03971v1/pass1_Different_Graph_Datasets.svg" id="A4.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/303;" width="548" height="303"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Success Rate of <span id="A4.F7.sf2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Dr.GRPO</span> on held out test sets of different levels of difficulty. The model manages to solve simpler variants (<span id="A4.F7.sf2.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#1F77B4;">Degree-3-Path-3</span> and <span id="A4.F7.sf2.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF8000;">Degree-5-Path-5</span>), but is unable to solve the harder <span id="A4.F7.sf2.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2CA02C;">Degree-10-Path-10</span> variant.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span><span id="A4.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Dr.GRPO</span> is able to solve easier instance of the graph search task. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</article>
</div>
